# =============================================================================
#  CSR Budgeting — environment template
#
#     cp .env.example .env      # then replace every change-me value
#
#  .env is gitignored; this template is not. Docker Compose reads .env for the
#  ${...} values in docker-compose.yml, and the app container receives them as
#  plain environment variables — no .env file is copied into any image.
#
#  Generate a real password with:
#     openssl rand -hex 32        (Linux/macOS/Git Bash)
# =============================================================================

# --- PostgreSQL (required) ---------------------------------------------------
# Used by the postgres service and injected into the app container.
POSTGRES_USER=csr_app
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-generate-with-openssl-rand-hex-32
POSTGRES_DB=csr_budgeting

# Host port for the database. Published on 127.0.0.1 only; raise it if another
# PostgreSQL already owns 5432 on this machine.
POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=5432

# --- Application -------------------------------------------------------------
# Host port the web UI is published on. The container always listens on 3000.
APP_HOST_PORT=3000
# --- Environment gate (Dev Seed Login, disposable dev feature) ---------------
# The /dev endpoints (GET /dev/seed-users) and the login-page panel are for a
# developer's machine: local Docker, bare `bun run dev`, or `vercel dev`. They
# are never mounted on a deployed Vercel function (production or preview), even
# when APP_ENV is unset. APP_ENV=production additionally disables them anywhere.
APP_ENV=development
# Demo-only override: DEV_SEED_LOGIN=1 turns the /dev endpoints on even on a
# deployed Vercel function AND provisions the 13 disposable test accounts at
# boot (server/db/initialize.ts), so the panel has identities to hand out.
# Any visitor can then read test passwords — demo/staging deployments only.
#DEV_SEED_LOGIN=1
# Frontend gate: the panel is shown unless VITE_APP_ENV is "production".
# vercel.json sets VITE_APP_ENV=production for Vercel builds, so nothing needs
# to be configured in the dashboard; leave this unset for local/Docker builds.
# VITE_APP_ENV=development

# --- Connection pool and timeouts (defaults shown; optional) -----------------
PG_POOL_MAX=10
PG_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
PG_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=60000
# How long the entrypoint waits for PostgreSQL before refusing to start.
DB_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# Set to 1 only on a pre-migrated database whose runtime cannot ship the
# migrations folder (e.g. a serverless bundle): the server then skips boot
# migrations and assumes the schema is current. Leave unset for Docker/dev.
#DB_SKIP_MIGRATIONS=0

# --- PostgreSQL server tuning (optional) -------------------------------------
# Sized for a small single-node deployment; raise these with the host's RAM.
POSTGRES_MAX_CONNECTIONS=100
POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS=256MB
POSTGRES_SLOW_QUERY_MS=1000

# --- Authentication (required) ----------------------------------------------
# Session security is built on HS256-signed access tokens + rotating refresh
# tokens in httpOnly cookies. The secret below is the only thing standing
# between an attacker and session forgery — treat it like the DB password.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Rotating this secret signs everyone out (tokens stop verifying). Change it
# after any suspicion of compromise; change passwords independently too.
AUTH_JWT_SECRET=change-me-openssl-rand-hex-32-minimum-32-chars

# Token lifetimes. Access = short (minutes); refresh = days. 15 min / 30 days
# are sensible interactive-app defaults.
AUTH_ACCESS_TTL_SECONDS=900
AUTH_REFRESH_TTL_DAYS=30

# Cookies: Secure is automatic in production (HTTPS). Keep SameSite=lax so
# normal cross-portal navigation keeps the session; use strict for maximum
# hardening when linking from other portals is not needed.
AUTH_COOKIE_SAMESITE=lax

# NOTE: there is no self-registration anywhere in this system. Accounts are
# created only by an admin from the role & permission manager (the «افزودن
# کاربر» action posts to /api/auth/users), in addition to the first-admin
# bootstrap below.

# First-admin bootstrap (optional). If unset, the first boot creates an
# account admin@localhost with a generated password printed ONCE to stderr.
# For a controlled deployment, set both values here and delete them from the
# file after the first login.
#AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_MOBILE=09120000000
#AUTH_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=

# --- Host-side tooling -------------------------------------------------------
# The app container gets its connection details from docker-compose.yml. These
# values are for scripts run on the host against the published port:
#     bun run db:check
#     bun run db:import:sqlite --dry-run
# Uncomment to use them (DATABASE_URL, when set, wins over the discrete PG* set).
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://csr_app:change-me@localhost:5432/csr_budgeting
# PGHOST=localhost
# PGPORT=5432
# PGUSER=csr_app
# PGPASSWORD=change-me-generate-with-openssl-rand-hex-32
# PGDATABASE=csr_budgeting
# PGSSLMODE=disable            # disable | require | verify-full | no-verify

# --- Managed / remote PostgreSQL (optional) ----------------------------------
# For a provider that terminates TLS (Neon, RDS, Cloud SQL, Supabase…), the URL
# form is the one to use; it carries host, port, credentials and sslmode:
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/dbname?sslmode=require
#
# Vercel Postgres / Neon integrations that inject POSTGRES_URL (or
# POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING / POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL) instead of DATABASE_URL also
# work, with a warning: setting DATABASE_URL explicitly is what keeps the
# configuration portable between hosts.

# --- Vercel (serverless) -----------------------------------------------------
# Vercel never reads this file: set these in Project → Settings → Environment
# Variables, for BOTH Production and Preview, then redeploy — a variable added
# after a deployment was built does not reach it.
# Vercel builds the client into public/ and deploys api/handler.ts as the
# Express function, so the database must be a hosted PostgreSQL (Neon,
# Supabase, Vercel Postgres…) reachable over the public internet. A
# localhost/Compose database is not reachable from a function.
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/dbname?sslmode=require
#   AUTH_JWT_SECRET=<openssl rand -hex 32>   # required: login/session signing
#   PG_POOL_MAX=3
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD from the Compose section above is NOT read by the app; on
# Vercel the connection is DATABASE_URL (or the platform-injected POSTGRES_URL).
# Every concurrent function instance opens its own pool, so keep PG_POOL_MAX
# small: the provider's connection ceiling is reached at (instances × pool).
# Do not set PORT or NODE_ENV — Vercel supplies both for functions.

# --- Demo role accounts (optional) -------------------------------------------
# The 13 RBAC roles can be seeded as REAL login accounts (09991000001–13), one
# per role, with the Dev* passwords listed in VERCEL.md. They are ordinary
# accounts — normal scrypt hash, no test flag, no plaintext — so the standard
# login form works anywhere, including a deployed Vercel function:
#
#   bun run db:seed:auth --init     # run on demand against DATABASE_URL
#
# Set this to create/refresh them at boot instead (on Vercel: Production and
# Preview, then redeploy):
# SEED_AUTH_ACCOUNTS=1
#
# SECURITY: the passwords are published in this repository. Keep the flag unset
# for a real deployment; rotate or deactivate the accounts after a demo.
# `bun run db:seed:dev` provisions the same mobiles as disposable test users
# for the local dev panel — never run it against a deployment.
